Answer: Runtime polymorphism occurs when a method call is resolved to the appropriate overridden instance method during program execution. For example, a parent reference can refer to a child object. If the child overrides an instance method, calling that method through the parent reference executes the child's implementation.

```java
class Parent {
```

void display() {

```java
System.out.println("Parent display");
}
}
class Child extends Parent {
@Override
```

void display() {

```java
System.out.println("Child display");
}
}
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Parent ref = new Child();
ref.display();
}
}
```

Output: Child display The reference is of type Parent, but the actual object is a Child. Because display() is overridden, the child's implementation executes.

Answer: ```java
class ParentOne {
static void display() {
System.out.println("Parent display");
}
}
class ChildOne extends ParentOne {
static void display() {
System.out.println("Child display");
}
}
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
ChildOne.display();
ParentOne.display();
}
}
```

Output: Child display Parent display Both classes declare a static display() method. Calling the method using each class name accesses the corresponding declaration.

Answer: Consider this example:

```java
class ParentOne {
static void display() {
System.out.println("Hello from Parent");
}
}
class ChildOne extends ParentOne {
static void display() {
System.out.println("Hello from Child");
}
}
public class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
ParentOne ref = new ChildOne();
ref.display();
}
}
```

Output: Hello from Parent Although ref refers to a ChildOne object, the reference variable is declared as ParentOne. Static method access is resolved using the reference's declared type in this example. The compiler may issue a warning because static methods should normally be accessed using the class name, such as ParentOne.display().
